WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.800
The year is 2019. I was a bright-eyed youth going&nbsp;
into my second year of college, and a couple high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.160
school friends I used to play Magic with moved&nbsp;
within walking distance of where I lived. We were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.960
all in one place once again, but none of us had&nbsp;
played much Magic recently—my friends each had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.080
single EDH deck they’d built in high school,&nbsp;
and I didn’t even have that, having recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.200
lost the mono-black removal pile I played with in&nbsp;
high school in a move. So what was the best way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.360
to play Magic under these circumstances and on a&nbsp;
broke college student budget? Revamp our old decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.160
and/or build new ones to match them in power? We&nbsp;
pondered this question until noticing a guild die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:43.680
that had come in a D&amp;D dice set my friend Connor&nbsp;
had bought. This die spawned an idea—what if,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.200
rather than building one or two new decks each, we&nbsp;
instead built a low-cost deck for each guild and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.160
rolled randomly to figure out what deck we’d play&nbsp;
with in a given game? This would allow us to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.800
a variety of different decks without straining&nbsp;
our meager budgets. Thus began the $25 deck pool.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.400
The deckbuilding began in an interesting place.&nbsp;
Though I knew the Sheoldred deck of my youth had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.040
problems, I was still a black player wanting to do&nbsp;
black player things, so I opted to build an Orzhov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.920
control deck. Putting Kambal in the command zone&nbsp;
offered a great way to keep the deck’s life total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.040
up while chipping away at opposing life totals,&nbsp;
and allowed the rest of the deck to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:28.640
removal, draw, and various lifegain-related combos&nbsp;
rather than trying to develop beaters. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.120
my friend Hans built a pretty brutal Azorius&nbsp;
control list with Noyan Dar in the command zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:39.680
and the fact that the pool began with two control&nbsp;
decks is a factor that shouldn’t be overlooked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.440
As other decks got fleshed out, this influence&nbsp;
can be seen—of the pool’s five midrange decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.280
three were some form of aristocrats, and another&nbsp;
was a land-focused deck that didn’t rely on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:55.960
keeping nonland permanents in play. The pool&nbsp;
also ended up containing a few more explosive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:01.360
combo-oriented decks, and these were likewise&nbsp;
well-suited to handle control, consisting of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:07.400
graveyard-centric artifacts deck, a big mana deck,&nbsp;
and a deck with a number of its own counterspells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.240
Finally, we have what ended up being the&nbsp;
hardest color combination to build a $25 deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:19.160
for—Dimir. For my first attempt, I opted for an&nbsp;
artifact tokens list with Rona as the commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.440
a midrange deck that could hopefully be flexible&nbsp;
enough to survive in a wide range of scenarios.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.840
So this was the first pool we had, and we played&nbsp;
about 30 games with it before going back to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:35.400
major changes. So, how did these decks pan&nbsp;
out? In a word—interestingly. I kept data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.560
on all the games, and the Orzhov deck garnered&nbsp;
an eye-watering 54% winrate, its potent mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:47.280
of lifegain, suppressive elements, and explosive&nbsp;
combos proving too much for most opposing decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:54.320
Among the other decks, winrates varied from 30%&nbsp;
all the way down to 0%, and as the 0 of the group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.280
the Niv-Mizzet combo deck got switched out for&nbsp;
an artifact-oriented combo deck that uses its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:05.360
commander, Jhoira, to draw into combo pieces.&nbsp;
In addition to these wide power differences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.560
a lot of the decks just felt kinda flat and&nbsp;
lifeless, generic midrange decks doing generic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.520
midrange things. For this reason, the Selesnya&nbsp;
midrange deck and the Tatyova lands deck ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:22.240
up getting swapped out for a cats aggro deck&nbsp;
and a Vannifar toolbox deck, both decks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.080
would hopefully bring a bit more zing to the&nbsp;
pool than their predecessors had. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:32.480
the Dimir Rona deck unfortunately just did not&nbsp;
work, and looking at the old decklist I can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.120
why—it was an unfocused mishmash of vaguely&nbsp;
artifact-oriented stuff, and it mostly ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:44.040
up feeling toothless above all else. I actually&nbsp;
retuned the deck to be a proliferate deck about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:50.240
halfway through this period of 30 games, and&nbsp;
that was decidedly not an improvement for it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.720
it was at this point where I scrapped the deck and&nbsp;
followed an idea from my friend Connor to rebuild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.440
it as a Lasav deck, a sort of graveyard-based&nbsp;
combo deck that would use its commander to swing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.200
big with infect through a card called Vector&nbsp;
Asp. With that, a commander swap for Azorius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.840
and some tuning to all decks, we were&nbsp;
ready to try out another round of games.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.600
After playing another 30 games with the updated&nbsp;
pool, the changes seemed to be working out pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.480
well overall. There was a more even spread&nbsp;
among the winrates, and the new combo decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.320
were working out just fine. Lazav was sitting&nbsp;
comfortably mid-pack in terms of winrate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:28.880
its mixture of controlling and combo elements able&nbsp;
to pull through wins often enough. Intriguingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.760
Orzhov won literally none of its 11 games within&nbsp;
this period, mostly due to players pressuring it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.840
down first in most of its games. However, its&nbsp;
place of dominance was taken by a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:46.960
deck—cats. Commanding a 42% winrate, the cats&nbsp;
deck ended up being a truly ruthless aggro deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.040
with the deck’s commander-pumped cats putting on&nbsp;
heavy pressure as early as turn 2. The deck was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:56.840
also able to fit in enough draw to grind into&nbsp;
the lategame, and this one-two punch was too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:01.960
much for a lot of the slower decks in the pool.&nbsp;
With strong performances from some faster decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.840
the Golgari and Rakdos aristocrats decks&nbsp;
were deemed a bit too slow and bland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.560
and ended up getting swapped out for&nbsp;
two more aggressive, dynamic decks:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.040
a Grismold tokens deck and a discard deck&nbsp;
with Malfegor as the commander. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.240
in the wake of cats making a large splash&nbsp;
within the pool, several slower decks were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.760
modified to have additional lifegain, board&nbsp;
wipes, and creatures to block with. Within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:29.840
these 10 decks a meaningful metagame had been&nbsp;
developed, a metagame where lacking ways to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.400
off creature-based aggression was something&nbsp;
that would cause a deck to win fewer games.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:38.080
The next period with these ten decks is&nbsp;
interesting because of just how long it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.400
lasted with no major alterations to the deck&nbsp;
pool. Though this period lasted just under 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.800
months of real world time, there happened to&nbsp;
be some societal factors that hindered other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:55.560
forms of socialization, so we ended up playing&nbsp;
86 games in that time frame. This fact itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.280
indicates that this pool was actually pretty good,&nbsp;
both in terms of deck balance and in terms of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:05.080
the decks felt. This is also the first time I&nbsp;
had a big enough sample size to consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.400
stats halfway reliable, and balance-wise, the&nbsp;
decks ended up grouping into three clusters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.240
terms of winrate. The most powerful deck deck in&nbsp;
this period was the Simic toolbox midrange deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.720
winning a third of its games, and this is likely&nbsp;
because it’s just a very well-rounded deck. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.800
able to keep up a moderately-above-average winrate&nbsp;
after the pool’s metagame had been figured out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.440
as opposed to the Orzhov and Selesnya decks which&nbsp;
had each had their moments of extreme dominance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:36.440
before eventually being adapted to by other decks.&nbsp;
Speaking of those, the next cluster contained five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:42.560
decks that all had a high 20s win percentage:&nbsp;
the Orzhov control deck, the Selesnya cats deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.520
the Golgari Grismold deck, the Azorius control&nbsp;
deck, and the Gruul big mana deck. The lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:54.320
cluster was in the high teens—decidedly not a&nbsp;
terrible winrate, I feel it should be noted, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.920
not a great one either—and this group contained&nbsp;
the Boros eggs deck, the Rakdos discard deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.680
and the two combo decks. Despite holding&nbsp;
up decently in the previous round of games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:10.000
both combo decks put on a pretty lackluster&nbsp;
performance in these 86 games, and I chalk this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.120
up to two factors. For one, their play patterns&nbsp;
got figured out—for the Lazav deck, this meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.880
that people could know roughly how quickly the&nbsp;
deck would achieve its combo, and also what weak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.040
points should be targeted when in order to disrupt&nbsp;
the deck. The other factor is that the pool had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.880
become significantly more aggressive as the number&nbsp;
of aggro decks increased and as the slower decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.760
added more creatures to stave off pressure. And&nbsp;
ultimately, the Lasav deck’s biggest weakness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.360
is that it simply didn’t have the blockers to&nbsp;
keep away pressure for long enough to combo.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.068
So, what did we decide to do with this pool?&nbsp;
Well, I factored in placement in games into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.068 --> 00:07:49.280
calculation for purposes of deciding which decks&nbsp;
weren’t working, and my reasoning for this is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:53.640
a deck that doesn’t win much but is durable enough&nbsp;
to stick around awhile is more fun than a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:58.760
that doesn’t win much and also gets knocked out&nbsp;
first each round. The Radkos discard deck, and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:03.720
a lesser extent the Boros eggs deck, was grindy&nbsp;
and interactive enough to stay alive, whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:10.120
the Dimir and Izzet decks had a tendency to get&nbsp;
knocked out first, especially the Lasav deck. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:14.760
after three different iterations of slower Dimir&nbsp;
decks being tried out, the Dimir Lasav deck was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:20.640
replaced with a rogue-based aggro deck with Sygg,&nbsp;
River Cutthroat as the commander. It had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:25.880
what it had tried and failed to survive against.&nbsp;
With this Sygg deck and a new Izzet spellslinger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:31.960
deck, the pool entered its finished state. We’ve&nbsp;
actually only played about 25 games with the pool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:37.480
since these early 2021 updates, and this is for&nbsp;
the simple reason that we each found things to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.440
with our lives aside from sitting inside playing&nbsp;
commander games. Don’t get me wrong, I’m glad we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:47.160
did this final update, and the Sygg deck I built&nbsp;
for it is one that I still occasionally pull out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:53.520
to this day, but most of these decks have probably&nbsp;
seen less than 10 games in the past two years. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.880
fervor we had for building, tracking, and updating&nbsp;
these decks was greatly fueled by the fact that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:05.120
basically all took place during 2020 and the first&nbsp;
half of 2021. And the funny thing is, the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:09.800
that I had this major period of EDH deckbuilding&nbsp;
during my college years almost certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:15.880
contributed to my decision to begin making videos&nbsp;
about the format. So in a sense, Salubrious Snail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:21.680
is brought to you by the Covid-19 pandemic.&nbsp;
Garbage sponsor honestly, would not recommend.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.000
So, what is there to take away from this&nbsp;
little fable I’ve weaved for you? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.560
to start with I would say that if you’re&nbsp;
tempted to make your own pool of decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:36.000
do it! It’s a super fun process, and the creation&nbsp;
of a little metagame within these is neat. In this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:42.200
video I probably made this process seem highly&nbsp;
analytical, but realistically we mostly just went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.480
with what we felt like, with the constraint of two&nbsp;
rules—decks should be a consistent power level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:52.240
and decks should do something unique that other&nbsp;
decks need to consider, both during games and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.680
during the deckbuilding process. There are a&nbsp;
handful of somewhat more generic decks in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:03.560
pool such as Simic, Dimir, and Izzet, but the&nbsp;
other 7 are extremely evocative and put forth a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:09.040
specific threat that requires consideration. The&nbsp;
Rakdos deck tries to render the table hellbent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:14.080
by turn 6, the Gruul deck inevitably explodes&nbsp;
out of control if it stays alive long enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:19.120
the Azorius deck is a truly oppressive control&nbsp;
deck, the Grismold deck injects an aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:24.120
dose of voltron into the pool, and the Orzhov&nbsp;
deck can dominate late games with its mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.360
of board wipes and lifegain-based combos. I dug&nbsp;
around some old discord messages while scripting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:34.240
for this video, and the specificity of the&nbsp;
decks in this pool can be seen in how me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:39.960
Hans, and the other players would talk about the&nbsp;
decks. Can this survive X, can this pressure Y,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:44.960
does this fold to Z? If these decks were all&nbsp;
generic midrange decks, this thought process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:51.160
would be wholly unnecessary, but since these&nbsp;
decks are so punchy and evocative in what they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.960
they demand consideration. So I guess what&nbsp;
I’m saying here is basically just that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:01.800
you build a set of decks like this, don’t let any&nbsp;
of them be overly weak or overly boring, and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:06.920
the courage to be able to dismantle a deck you&nbsp;
like if it ends up being one of these things.

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:12.000
As a final point, this process of building decks&nbsp;
formulated a lot my understanding of the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.240
format, and when I started playing with decks&nbsp;
outside this pool in the first half of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:21.200
I had kind of a rude awakening where&nbsp;
most of the games I played were way less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.120
dynamic and interesting than the games&nbsp;
with these $25 decks. My first Magic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:31.160
the Gathering video was partially inspired by&nbsp;
this, this experience of playing against bland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:35.880
formulaic midrange decks without enough&nbsp;
interaction to make for a dynamic game. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:40.080
while I’ve had plenty more interesting games since&nbsp;
then, I try to keep the mindset that I had when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.600
building those $25 decks, which is to say, try&nbsp;
to present a specific problem for your opponents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.760
and be prepared for if your opponents present&nbsp;
a problem for you. To me, multiplayer games are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:55.200
more fun when people have to think about their&nbsp;
opponents, rather than when everybody is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:59.240
racing engines against each other like we’re&nbsp;
all playing some kind of second-rate Eurogame.

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:04.160
Thanks for watching! I linked a folder in the&nbsp;
description that contains the final versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:10.960
of all 10 of these decks, though some cards have&nbsp;
gone up in price in the past few years. I’m also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:15.520
releasing an endnote over on my Patreon where&nbsp;
I discuss the Orzhov deck in a bit more detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:19.920
so if that interests you you can head on&nbsp;
over there and throw some money at me.

